WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.772
We will use Chroma as the main hands-on vector store because it is easy to run locally.

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:09.667
The code creates a persistent client and a collection.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:14.927
Then we insert our existing chunk IDs, chunk text, metadata, and embeddings.

00:00:14.927 --> 00:00:21.552
Notice that we are not changing the embedding model or rebuilding the documents.

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:27.599
We are moving the same objects from our in-memory NumPy workflow into a persistent vector store.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.612
When a user asks a question, we create the query embedding and send it to Chroma.

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:37.327
Chroma returns the nearest documents and metadata.

00:00:37.327 --> 00:00:42.052
The example also includes a metadata filter for topic.

00:00:42.052 --> 00:00:46.652
This is a good point to compare the result with the NumPy version.

00:00:46.652 --> 00:00:49.287
The architecture should feel familiar.

00:00:49.287 --> 00:00:56.497
If it does, that means you are understanding RAG at the right level: the storage implementation changed,

00:00:56.497 --> 00:00:58.872
but the overall pipeline did not.
